1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,005
At the Corner Cafe, Asha wanted a summary
for three days.

2
00:00:04,025 --> 00:00:06,720
So she copied the same lines three times.

3
00:00:06,767 --> 00:00:08,070
Then she added tax.

4
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:12,805
She changed the Monday copy and the
Tuesday copy, and missed Friday.

5
00:00:12,825 --> 00:00:15,591
Copying code means copying its mistakes.

6
00:00:16,187 --> 00:00:19,347
This is Code Quest, Chapter 16: functions.

7
00:00:19,367 --> 00:00:21,847
Write it once, use it many times.

8
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:27,236
And an AI assistant will write a function
for Asha, and promise exactly what it

9
00:00:27,256 --> 00:00:27,713
prints.

10
00:00:27,733 --> 00:00:29,351
Your checks will decide.

11
00:00:29,953 --> 00:00:33,107
So here is today's mission: the day
report.

12
00:00:33,127 --> 00:00:36,133
Three small functions, each with one job.

13
00:00:36,167 --> 00:00:40,567
And three short lines at the bottom print
a report for three different days.

14
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,835
If the tax changes, Asha edits one line.

15
00:00:43,855 --> 00:00:45,055
Let's build it.

16
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:50,690
Write functions, return values and
docstrings, and read a longer traceback.

17
00:00:50,733 --> 00:00:55,805
And write a specification, ask an AI for
the code, and test it with your own

18
00:00:55,825 --> 00:00:56,274
checks.

19
00:00:56,850 --> 00:00:59,131
A function is a named block of code.

20
00:00:59,151 --> 00:01:02,392
You write it once, and run it whenever you
need it.

21
00:01:02,412 --> 00:01:05,780
Fix a mistake inside it, and every call
gets the fix.

22
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:11,254
You have used functions since the first
chapters: print, len, round and input.

23
00:01:11,274 --> 00:01:12,868
Now you write your own.

24
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:15,149
Here is a whole function.

25
00:01:15,169 --> 00:01:19,620
The def line gives it a name, with tax,
and one parameter, price.

26
00:01:19,667 --> 00:01:21,733
The indented line is the body.

27
00:01:21,753 --> 00:01:25,697
Return hands a value back to whoever
called the function.

28
00:01:25,733 --> 00:01:27,344
The last line is a call.

29
00:01:27,364 --> 00:01:32,168
A value goes in, and the returned value
comes out and is stored in tea.

30
00:01:32,787 --> 00:01:33,561
Five words.

31
00:01:33,581 --> 00:01:37,893
To define means to write the function, and
nothing runs yet.

32
00:01:37,913 --> 00:01:41,647
To call means to run it, with its name and
brackets.

33
00:01:41,667 --> 00:01:47,072
A parameter is a name in the def line, an
argument is the value in a call, and the

34
00:01:47,092 --> 00:01:49,190
return value is what comes back.

35
00:01:49,787 --> 00:01:52,840
Think of a recipe card in the cafe
kitchen.

36
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:55,913
The cook follows the same steps every
time.

37
00:01:55,933 --> 00:01:59,487
Today's ingredients go in, and one dish
comes out.

38
00:01:59,533 --> 00:02:03,998
But unlike a cook, a function cannot taste
the dish and adjust it.

39
00:02:04,018 --> 00:02:07,192
It does exactly what its body says, every
time.

40
00:02:07,787 --> 00:02:08,612
Quick check.

41
00:02:08,632 --> 00:02:10,796
In this def line, what is name?

42
00:02:22,147 --> 00:02:22,267
B.

43
00:02:22,287 --> 00:02:23,110
A parameter.

44
00:02:23,130 --> 00:02:25,146
It is a name in the def line.

45
00:02:25,166 --> 00:02:29,501
The value you pass when you call the
function is the argument.

46
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:31,207
Take a breather.

47
00:02:31,227 --> 00:02:33,214
Here is what you know so far.

48
00:02:33,234 --> 00:02:35,775
Next, Asha writes her first function.

49
00:02:40,517 --> 00:02:41,010
Step 1.

50
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:44,017
Line 2 defines a function called welcome.

51
00:02:44,037 --> 00:02:47,537
It has no parameters, so the brackets are
empty.

52
00:02:47,567 --> 00:02:51,413
Lines 3 and 4 are its body, indented by
four spaces.

53
00:02:51,433 --> 00:02:54,387
Defining the function does not run them.

54
00:02:54,433 --> 00:02:56,628
Lines 6 and 7 are two calls.

55
00:02:56,648 --> 00:02:59,001
Each call runs the whole body.

56
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:10,668
Four.

57
00:03:10,688 --> 00:03:15,356
Each call prints the welcome and the
special, so the two lines appear twice.

58
00:03:15,953 --> 00:03:16,499
Step 2.

59
00:03:16,519 --> 00:03:19,654
Now the def line has a parameter, name.

60
00:03:19,674 --> 00:03:22,403
Line 3 uses it inside an f string.

61
00:03:22,433 --> 00:03:25,789
Line 5 calls greet with the argument Omar.

62
00:03:25,809 --> 00:03:28,522
In that call, name refers to Omar.

63
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:40,727
Hello, Lina!

64
00:03:40,747 --> 00:03:42,027
Your tea is ready.

65
00:03:42,047 --> 00:03:44,916
In the second call, name refers to Lina.

66
00:03:45,517 --> 00:03:46,046
Step 3.

67
00:03:46,066 --> 00:03:51,937
Return ends the function and hands a value
back to the line that called it.

68
00:03:51,967 --> 00:03:55,927
Line 5 stores the returned value in tea,
and line 6 prints it.

69
00:03:55,967 --> 00:03:59,239
Line 7 prints a returned value directly.

70
00:03:59,259 --> 00:04:03,767
Line 8 compares one with 3, so it prints
True or False.

71
00:04:15,147 --> 00:04:16,511
4.4, and True.

72
00:04:16,531 --> 00:04:22,047
With tax, 2.75 becomes about 3.03, which
is more than 3.

73
00:04:22,067 --> 00:04:26,627
And line 6 prints 3.85, the value stored
in tea.

74
00:04:26,647 --> 00:04:31,589
A returned value can be stored, printed or
compared.

75
00:04:32,187 --> 00:04:32,643
Step 4.

76
00:04:32,663 --> 00:04:34,954
Print shows a value on the screen.

77
00:04:34,974 --> 00:04:37,741
Return hands a value back to the program.

78
00:04:37,761 --> 00:04:39,237
They are not the same.

79
00:04:39,267 --> 00:04:43,615
A function without return hands back a
special value: None.

80
00:04:43,635 --> 00:04:45,467
It means no value at all.

81
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:48,302
This function prints the price with tax.

82
00:04:48,322 --> 00:04:51,618
Line 5 stores whatever it hands back in
result.

83
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:04,158
Result is None.

84
00:05:04,178 --> 00:05:09,350
The function printed 3.85 on the screen,
but it returned nothing.

85
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:13,228
So if the caller needs the value, the
function must return it.

86
00:05:13,817 --> 00:05:14,375
Step 5.

87
00:05:14,395 --> 00:05:19,747
Line 2 has two parameters, separated by a
comma: price and count.

88
00:05:19,767 --> 00:05:25,168
Line 3 is a docstring: a string on the
first line of the body that says what the

89
00:05:25,188 --> 00:05:26,117
function does.

90
00:05:26,167 --> 00:05:29,681
In a call, the arguments fill the
parameters in order.

91
00:05:29,701 --> 00:05:33,411
The first argument is the price, the
second is the count.

92
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:45,282
8.25.

93
00:05:45,302 --> 00:05:51,550
The price is 2.75, the count is 3, and
2.75 times 3 is 8.25.

94
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:54,516
And line 8 prints the docstring.

95
00:05:54,536 --> 00:06:00,939
Python keeps it under the name doc, with
two underscores on each side.

96
00:06:01,553 --> 00:06:02,362
Quick check.

97
00:06:02,382 --> 00:06:05,191
The function area is called with 3 and 5.

98
00:06:05,211 --> 00:06:06,226
What is height?

99
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:17,725
D.

100
00:06:17,745 --> 00:06:17,891
5.

101
00:06:17,911 --> 00:06:21,117
Arguments fill the parameters in order.

102
00:06:21,137 --> 00:06:23,764
Width gets 3, and height gets 5.

103
00:06:24,387 --> 00:06:24,943
Breather.

104
00:06:24,963 --> 00:06:27,119
Relax your shoulders for a moment.

105
00:06:27,139 --> 00:06:32,431
Next, functions that call other functions,
and what happens when one of them fails.

106
00:06:37,183 --> 00:06:37,726
Step 6.

107
00:06:37,746 --> 00:06:40,699
A function can call another function.

108
00:06:40,719 --> 00:06:43,993
Day total starts a total at 0, on line 6.

109
00:06:44,033 --> 00:06:50,843
For every price, line 8 calls with tax,
and adds the returned value to the total.

110
00:06:50,867 --> 00:06:53,725
Line 9 returns the total, rounded.

111
00:06:53,745 --> 00:06:56,942
Line 11 prints 11.28 for three prices.

112
00:06:57,553 --> 00:07:04,513
Now line 11 passes a list with one price
written as text: 4.0, in quotation marks.

113
00:07:04,533 --> 00:07:08,621
Day total passes each price to with tax,
one at a time.

114
00:07:08,641 --> 00:07:13,252
Chapter 9 promised that tracebacks grow
longer with functions.

115
00:07:13,272 --> 00:07:14,148
Here is one.

116
00:07:25,513 --> 00:07:27,102
In with tax, on line 3.

117
00:07:27,122 --> 00:07:30,458
Python cannot multiply text by a decimal
number.

118
00:07:30,478 --> 00:07:35,633
The error report lists every call on the
way, one block for each function.

119
00:07:35,667 --> 00:07:38,793
Read it from the bottom up, as in Chapter
9.

120
00:07:38,813 --> 00:07:40,937
The last line names the error.

121
00:07:40,957 --> 00:07:46,157
The block directly above it shows where it
happened: line 3, in with tax.

122
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:50,690
The block above that shows who called it:
line 8, in day total.

123
00:07:50,733 --> 00:07:54,016
And the top block is line 11, the main
program.

124
00:07:54,036 --> 00:07:58,373
Most recent call last means the deepest
call is at the bottom.

125
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:02,738
The cause is on line 11: the price in
quotation marks.

126
00:08:03,317 --> 00:08:04,618
Back to the mission.

127
00:08:04,638 --> 00:08:07,394
Each function has one job and a docstring.

128
00:08:07,414 --> 00:08:10,897
With tax, on lines 2 to 4, adds the tax to
one price.

129
00:08:10,933 --> 00:08:14,420
Day total, on lines 6 to 11, adds up a
whole day.

130
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:16,853
It calls with tax for every price.

131
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:22,429
Report, on lines 13 to 15, prints one
line: the day, the number of sales, and

132
00:08:22,449 --> 00:08:24,230
the total from day total.

133
00:08:24,267 --> 00:08:29,267
Line 19 asks for the report of a day with
no sales at all: an empty list.

134
00:08:40,647 --> 00:08:42,261
Friday, 0 sales, 0.00.

135
00:08:42,281 --> 00:08:46,717
The loop in day total runs zero times, so
the total stays 0.

136
00:08:46,767 --> 00:08:52,592
And Monday and Tuesday get their own lines
from the same three functions.

137
00:08:52,612 --> 00:08:56,355
To change the tax, Asha edits one line:
line 4.

138
00:08:56,375 --> 00:08:57,717
Mission complete!

139
00:08:57,767 --> 00:09:02,311
Next, Asha wants a function for her bill
splitter from Chapter 5.

140
00:09:02,331 --> 00:09:06,806
This time, an AI assistant writes the
code, one stage at a time.

141
00:09:07,383 --> 00:09:10,557
This chapter's AI skill is the
specification.

142
00:09:10,577 --> 00:09:16,376
A specification says a function's name,
its parameters, what it returns, and a few

143
00:09:16,396 --> 00:09:18,363
examples with exact results.

144
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:21,794
You write the specification and your own
checks.

145
00:09:21,814 --> 00:09:23,430
The AI writes the code.

146
00:09:23,450 --> 00:09:26,559
And your checks decide if the code is
right.

147
00:09:27,153 --> 00:09:28,184
Stage 1: plan.

148
00:09:28,204 --> 00:09:32,911
Asha describes the job: a total, a number
of people, and a tip.

149
00:09:32,931 --> 00:09:36,363
She asks for a specification, and no code
yet.

150
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:42,679
The AI gives a name, three parameters, the
return value, and three examples.

151
00:09:42,699 --> 00:09:49,476
Asha checks example 1 by hand: 40 plus 10
percent is 44, and 44 shared by 4 is 11.

152
00:09:50,087 --> 00:09:51,237
Stage 2: write.

153
00:09:51,257 --> 00:09:55,917
Asha writes three check lines herself, one
for each example.

154
00:09:55,937 --> 00:10:01,767
Each prints the returned value, then
whether it equals the expected result.

155
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:08,281
She asks the AI to predict them for a
correct function: 11.0 True, 10.77 True,

156
00:10:08,301 --> 00:10:09,115
3.33 True.

157
00:10:09,135 --> 00:10:11,115
Asha predicted the same.

158
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:13,072
Stage 3: generate.

159
00:10:13,092 --> 00:10:19,246
Asha asks the AI to write the function,
and to say exactly what her f string line

160
00:10:19,266 --> 00:10:19,780
prints.

161
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:25,460
The AI writes three lines, and promises:
Each pays 11.00.

162
00:10:26,053 --> 00:10:30,054
Here is the AI's function with Asha's
line, line 6.

163
00:10:30,074 --> 00:10:32,892
Look at line 4, and remember step 4.

164
00:10:44,247 --> 00:10:44,438
No.

165
00:10:44,458 --> 00:10:48,531
Line 4 prints the share, and the function
returns nothing.

166
00:10:48,551 --> 00:10:50,507
So the call gives back None.

167
00:10:50,533 --> 00:10:56,365
The f string cannot format None with two
decimal places, so Python stops with a

168
00:10:56,385 --> 00:10:57,920
Type Error on line 6.

169
00:10:57,940 --> 00:11:02,068
The AI broke its own specification, and it
sounded sure.

170
00:11:02,687 --> 00:11:03,724
Stage 4: test.

171
00:11:03,744 --> 00:11:09,767
Asha tells the AI what happened, and asks
for a function that returns the value.

172
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:12,182
The AI gives a corrected version.

173
00:11:12,202 --> 00:11:14,002
It returns the share now.

174
00:11:14,022 --> 00:11:15,967
Asha does not trust it yet.

175
00:11:15,987 --> 00:11:18,296
She runs her three checks on it.

176
00:11:18,887 --> 00:11:20,724
Line 3 returns the share.

177
00:11:20,744 --> 00:11:24,587
Compare it with the specification: rounded
to cents.

178
00:11:24,633 --> 00:11:30,726
Lines 5 to 10 are Asha's checks: three
calls with the three examples, then three

179
00:11:30,746 --> 00:11:31,261
prints.

180
00:11:42,613 --> 00:11:43,934
Lines 9 and 10.

181
00:11:43,954 --> 00:11:45,991
The AI forgot to round.

182
00:11:46,011 --> 00:11:52,785
The second check is the Chapter 5 table:
43.07 shared by 4 is 10.7675, which

183
00:11:52,805 --> 00:11:55,199
Chapter 5 rounded to 10.77.

184
00:11:55,219 --> 00:11:57,523
The third has many digits.

185
00:11:57,567 --> 00:12:04,255
And the first check passed only by luck:
its exact answer has no extra digits.

186
00:12:04,275 --> 00:12:07,093
One passing check proves nothing.

187
00:12:07,113 --> 00:12:08,211
Run them all.

188
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:15,900
Asha adds round, with 2 places, on line 3,
as the specification says.

189
00:12:15,933 --> 00:12:18,799
Then she runs every check again.

190
00:12:18,819 --> 00:12:21,776
11.0 True, 10.77 True, 3.33 True.

191
00:12:21,796 --> 00:12:23,128
All three pass.

192
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:24,757
Stage 5: debug.

193
00:12:24,777 --> 00:12:29,692
Asha uses the function in her program, and
it stops with a Type Error.

194
00:12:29,712 --> 00:12:35,049
She pastes the code and the full error
report, and asks for the cause first.

195
00:12:35,653 --> 00:12:40,765
The AI reads the last line: share is
missing one required argument, tip

196
00:12:40,785 --> 00:12:41,343
percent.

197
00:12:41,367 --> 00:12:46,975
The function has three parameters, but
line 6 passes only two arguments.

198
00:12:46,995 --> 00:12:49,008
The fix: pass the tip too.

199
00:12:49,620 --> 00:12:54,153
Asha adds the tip on line 6, and runs it
again.

200
00:12:54,173 --> 00:12:55,509
Table 4, 11.0.

201
00:12:55,529 --> 00:12:57,253
The program works.

202
00:12:57,853 --> 00:12:59,116
Stage 6: improve.

203
00:12:59,136 --> 00:13:01,379
Asha asks for one improvement.

204
00:13:01,399 --> 00:13:07,490
And she adds a rule: do not change the
name, the parameters, or what the function

205
00:13:07,510 --> 00:13:08,093
returns.

206
00:13:08,133 --> 00:13:12,820
The AI suggests putting people first,
because it reads more naturally.

207
00:13:12,840 --> 00:13:14,770
It promises the same results.

208
00:13:15,387 --> 00:13:16,396
Asha tries it.

209
00:13:16,416 --> 00:13:18,454
Line 2 now has people first.

210
00:13:18,474 --> 00:13:23,085
Her check lines stay the same: they still
pass the total first.

211
00:13:34,447 --> 00:13:34,652
No.

212
00:13:34,672 --> 00:13:36,534
Every check prints False.

213
00:13:36,554 --> 00:13:41,877
The calls pass 40 as people and 4 as the
total, so the shares are tiny.

214
00:13:41,900 --> 00:13:45,671
The AI broke Asha's rule: it changed the
parameters.

215
00:13:45,691 --> 00:13:51,140
Arguments fill parameters in order, so
every call now means something else.

216
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:52,817
Asha keeps her version.

217
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:58,910
Here is Asha's final function, with a
docstring from the specification, on line

218
00:13:58,930 --> 00:13:59,050
3.

219
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:03,430
She adds the docstring, so she runs every
check again.

220
00:14:03,450 --> 00:14:04,800
True, True, True.

221
00:14:04,833 --> 00:14:10,222
And line 12 uses it in her bill splitter:
each pays 11.00.

222
00:14:10,820 --> 00:14:11,666
Quick check.

223
00:14:11,686 --> 00:14:15,059
An AI wrote a function from your
specification.

224
00:14:15,079 --> 00:14:17,008
Who decides if it is right?

225
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:28,508
C.

226
00:14:28,528 --> 00:14:30,659
Your checks, run on its code.

227
00:14:30,679 --> 00:14:32,883
The AI's promise is not proof.

228
00:14:33,487 --> 00:14:34,086
Breather.

229
00:14:34,106 --> 00:14:39,457
Asha wrote a specification and her own
checks, and caught three AI mistakes: a

230
00:14:39,477 --> 00:14:44,070
print instead of a return, a missing
round, and swapped parameters.

231
00:14:48,817 --> 00:14:49,534
Mistake 1.

232
00:14:49,554 --> 00:14:53,734
Line 2 calls welcome, but the def comes
later, on line 4.

233
00:14:53,754 --> 00:14:58,597
Python runs from top to bottom, so it does
not know that name yet.

234
00:14:58,633 --> 00:15:00,920
Python stops with a Name Error.

235
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:04,045
The fix: move the call below the function.

236
00:15:04,653 --> 00:15:05,510
Now you try.

237
00:15:05,530 --> 00:15:09,529
Look closely at line 5, and remember what
a call needs.

238
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:22,680
The brackets are missing.

239
00:15:22,700 --> 00:15:27,339
Without them, tea refers to the function
itself, not to a price.

240
00:15:27,359 --> 00:15:29,960
Line 6 tries to add 1 to a function.

241
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,832
The fix: call it with an argument, such as
with tax of 3.5.

242
00:15:35,453 --> 00:15:39,851
Mistake 3 is step 4 again, and the AI's
first mistake.

243
00:15:39,871 --> 00:15:43,613
The function prints 3.85, but it returns
None.

244
00:15:43,633 --> 00:15:46,323
So line 5 tries to add 1 to None.

245
00:15:46,343 --> 00:15:48,787
None Type is the type of None.

246
00:15:48,807 --> 00:15:51,497
The fix: return instead of print.

247
00:15:52,120 --> 00:15:52,928
Mistake 4.

248
00:15:52,948 --> 00:15:55,745
Look at the indentation of line 6.

249
00:15:55,765 --> 00:15:58,230
The return is inside the loop.

250
00:15:58,267 --> 00:16:02,690
A return ends the whole function at once,
even in the middle of a loop.

251
00:16:14,047 --> 00:16:14,337
3.5.

252
00:16:14,357 --> 00:16:19,532
The loop added the first price, and then
return ended the function.

253
00:16:19,552 --> 00:16:22,557
There is no error, only a wrong answer.

254
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:27,703
The fix: move return four spaces to the
left, so it runs after the loop has

255
00:16:27,723 --> 00:16:28,318
finished.

256
00:16:28,920 --> 00:16:29,774
Last breather.

257
00:16:29,794 --> 00:16:33,892
Now you know the four function mistakes
that catch most beginners.

258
00:16:38,617 --> 00:16:39,012
First.

259
00:16:39,032 --> 00:16:42,886
Asha wants a function that gives members
10 percent off.

260
00:16:42,906 --> 00:16:48,283
Write its specification first: the return
value, and three examples, one for a

261
00:16:48,303 --> 00:16:49,044
non-member.

262
00:17:00,413 --> 00:17:02,804
Then write the function, and your checks.

263
00:17:03,420 --> 00:17:03,849
Second.

264
00:17:03,869 --> 00:17:07,251
A function can return two values at once,
as a tuple.

265
00:17:07,271 --> 00:17:10,589
How would you unpack them in the line that
calls it?

266
00:17:21,947 --> 00:17:25,783
Write a day summary function that returns
both, and test it.

267
00:17:26,383 --> 00:17:27,220
Let's recap.

268
00:17:27,240 --> 00:17:32,856
Def defines a function, a call runs it,
and parameters receive the arguments in

269
00:17:32,876 --> 00:17:33,284
order.

270
00:17:33,304 --> 00:17:35,353
Without return, you get None.

271
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:38,014
A docstring says what the function does.

272
00:17:38,034 --> 00:17:40,780
A long traceback reads from the bottom up.

273
00:17:40,800 --> 00:17:45,201
And with AI: write the specification and
your checks first.

274
00:17:45,221 --> 00:17:46,550
The checks decide.

275
00:17:47,153 --> 00:17:48,258
One last check.

276
00:17:48,278 --> 00:17:52,306
This function calculates n times 2, but
has no return.

277
00:17:52,326 --> 00:17:55,005
What does print of double of 4 show?

278
00:18:06,380 --> 00:18:06,519
A.

279
00:18:06,539 --> 00:18:06,915
None.

280
00:18:06,935 --> 00:18:10,563
The body calculates 8, but hands nothing
back.

281
00:18:11,153 --> 00:18:12,472
Mission complete!

282
00:18:12,492 --> 00:18:16,253
You earned 100 XP and the Function Forger
badge.

283
00:18:18,483 --> 00:18:22,671
Now it is your turn: eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

284
00:18:22,691 --> 00:18:27,863
Three warm-up tasks at 10 XP, and three
apply tasks at 20.

285
00:18:27,900 --> 00:18:33,038
And two stretch tasks at 40, including an
AI's member price to fix.

286
00:18:33,058 --> 00:18:36,272
Try each one before you open the solution.

287
00:18:36,850 --> 00:18:38,249
Asha's functions work.

288
00:18:38,269 --> 00:18:41,734
But inside day total there is a variable
called total.

289
00:18:41,754 --> 00:18:43,992
Can the rest of the program see it?

290
00:18:44,012 --> 00:18:46,250
And how big should one function be?

291
00:18:46,300 --> 00:18:51,650
In Chapter 17, you will learn where
variables live, and how to design small

292
00:18:51,670 --> 00:18:53,870
functions that each do one job.

293
00:18:53,900 --> 00:18:55,630
Do the warm-up tasks now.

294
00:18:55,650 --> 00:18:57,590
They take about ten minutes.

295
00:18:57,633 --> 00:18:59,592
See you in Chapter 17.
